name issue_24_timing_v2_clean_link
describe 2.5 Mbps link, 25 ms one-way, no impairments, every v2 switch and libwebrtc's receive timing (timing v2): video delay at libwebrtc's level (issue #24)
# `issue_124_clean_link_v2` plus `timing v2`: the Owner's `clean_link` video e2e target for #24 (2026-10-20 15:07 UTC).
# Measured on main b119ce8, seeds 1-40 (ours timing v1 / ours timing v2; libwebrtc from parity-data of main b119ce8,
# issue_124_clean_link_v2, 10 seeds, with the receiver RTT from #208):
#   video.e2e_delay_p50_ms        59-63 (mean 61.6) / 71-79 (mean 74.9) ; libwebrtc 74.8
#   video.e2e_delay_p95_ms        81-180 (mean 118.5) / 93-180 (mean 124.6) ; libwebrtc 103-165 (mean 121.4)
#   video.time_to_first_frame_ms  67-68 / 51-52 ; libwebrtc 53.7
#   video.freeze_count            0 / 0 ; libwebrtc 0
# timing v1 renders about 13 ms earlier than libwebrtc; timing v2 (VCMTiming, JitterEstimator and
# VideoStreamBufferController's release, PR #207) holds frames as libwebrtc does. The p95 is set by the
# bottleneck queue (cc.queue_p95_ms 43-142, mean 79.3, both timings; libwebrtc 72.0), not by receive timing.
# On plain `clean_link` (every switch v1) the e2e p95 is 68 against libwebrtc's 123: that gap is the cc v1
# queue (23 against 74 ms) and closes with the flip (#61), not here.
# The lines below lock the level reached on seeds 1-40, in both directions.
cc v2
pacer v2
allocator v2
packetization v2
audio v2
timing v2
duration 30s
at 0s capacity=2500kbps delay=25ms queue=300ms
expect video.e2e_delay_p50_ms >= 68
expect video.e2e_delay_p50_ms < 85
expect video.e2e_delay_p95_ms < 200
expect video.time_to_first_frame_ms < 60
expect video.freeze_count == 0
expect video.render_fps >= 29
